{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016da427Xej7Mf2whrhahXEQ","type":"tool_result","content":"342-| `reason` | string | Human-readable; structure lives in `code` and fields, not prose |\n343-\n344:### Work Item (the Epic 02 seam)\n345-\n346-The contract Epic 02's derivation pipeline consumes. Granularity (how many items one turn close queues) is tech design's call; the shape is not.\n347-\n348-| Field | Type | Values in this epic |\n349-|-------|------|---------------------|\n350-| `workItemId` | string | System-generated, unique per thread |\n351-| `owner` | string | `messages` or `turns` |\n352-| `kind` | string | `prompt_smoothing`, `tool_result_summary` (owner `messages`); `turn_derivation` (owner `turns`) |\n353-| `sourceRef` | object | `{ messageId }` for message-owned work; `{ turnId }` for turn-owned work |\n354-| `status` | string | Always `queued` in this epic; the lifecycle beyond queued belongs to Epic 02 |\n355-| `queuedAt` | string | Timestamp |\n356-\n357-### Read-back Shapes (verification surface)\n358-\n--\n381-\n382-## Tech Design Questions\n383-\n384:1. Work-item granularity at turn close: one `turn_derivation` item, or one per derived artifact? (Shape is fixed by the Work Item contract; count is design's call.)\n385-2. What transaction boundary guarantees the batch's atomicity across events, messages, turn state, and work items, and how does thread-file creation stay atomic with its registry row (no row without a file, no orphan file on a failed create)?\n386-3. What tokenizer provides the base-unit estimate, and how is its identity recorded so estimates remain interpretable if the base ever changes? (Tech arch names the intended choice; design confirms and pins it.)\n387-4. How are error classes and codes represented so SDK results and CLI output stay structurally identical?\n388-5. How does the two-open-turns corruption fixture get manufactured in tests without a public operation that can produce it?\n389-6. Where does the registry live and how is it initialized on first use? (Functionally: list and resolve work before any thread exists, returning empty/not-found.)\n390-\n391----\n392-\n332:| `queuedWork` | array | Each: `{ workItemId, owner, kind, sourceRef }` (shapes below) |\n350:| `workItemId` | string | System-generated, unique per thread |\n353:| `sourceRef` | object | `{ messageId }` for message-owned work; `{ turnId }` for turn-owned work |\n355:| `queuedAt` | string | Timestamp |\n365:- **Work item**: `{ workItemId, owner, kind, sourceRef, status: queued, queuedAt }`\n449:**Boundary / risk notes:** This story fixes the contract seam Epic 02 builds against — owner, kind, sourceRef, and `queued` status must match what the derivation epic consumes. Work items must queue inside the batch's atomic operation (a rejected batch queues nothing, a skipped event queues nothing).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ab0ad27-af7b-46f6-9ce5-aa43cce928f8","uuid":"4067e928-9c59-44d8-80ee-0536412954d1","timestamp":"2026-06-10T15:03:19.611Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"342-| `reason` | string | Human-readable; structure lives in `code` and fields, not prose |\n343-\n344:### Work Item (the Epic 02 seam)\n345-\n346-The contract Epic 02's derivation pipeline consumes. Granularity (how many items one turn close queues) is tech design's call; the shape is not.\n347-\n348-| Field | Type | Values in this epic |\n349-|-------|------|---------------------|\n350-| `workItemId` | string | System-generated, unique per thread |\n351-| `owner` | string | `messages` or `turns` |\n352-| `kind` | string | `prompt_smoothing`, `tool_result_summary` (owner `messages`); `turn_derivation` (owner `turns`) |\n353-| `sourceRef` | object | `{ messageId }` for message-owned work; `{ turnId }` for turn-owned work |\n354-| `status` | string | Always `queued` in this epic; the lifecycle beyond queued belongs to Epic 02 |\n355-| `queuedAt` | string | Timestamp |\n356-\n357-### Read-